{
  "info": {
    "name": "University Record Service",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json",
    "description": "University Record Service, tested through the Gateway ([http://localhost:8090](http://localhost:8090)). Its own ports are not published, so the Gateway is the only way in.\n\nSELF-CONTAINED: folder 0 registers three throwaway players (moderator, junior, stranger), logs them in, opens a real session, lets the junior join and starts it. Starting the session makes Session Service call AssignScopes on University Record over gRPC, so no grpcurl or manual step is needed. Run the collection top to bottom with the Collection Runner (each run registers fresh players).\n\nWHAT IT PROVES\n\n- Folder 1: POST /records checks the session with Session Service (gRPC): 201 for a running session, 404 unknown, 409 not started, 422/400/401 for bad input.\n    \n- Folder 2: GET /sessions/{id}/records/{applicantId} returns only the caller's assigned scopes (200), 403 for a player that is not in the session, 404 unknown applicant, 401 without a token.\n    \n- Folder 3 (Lab 3, grades 4-5): two replicas behind the Gateway; round-robin alternates the replicas; a record written through one replica is readable through the other (the service is instance-agnostic).\n    \n- Folder 4 (Lab 3, grades 6 and 9): circuit breaker, manual, see its description.\n    \n\nREQUIRES the service to answer with an X-Instance-Id header (the container id). Folder 3 fails without it.\n\nNOT COVERED HERE\n\n- GET /health: it is polled by Service Discovery inside the Compose network and is not routed by the Gateway. Check it with: docker compose logs discovery-service\n    \n- gRPC (AssignScopes, GetRecordSnapshot): exercised indirectly by starting the session."
  },
